<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Impi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as amabutho, ibutho</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Impi is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> word meaning war or combat, and by extension any body of men gathered for war; in English it usually means a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> regiment, an ibutho, or the army of the Zulu Kingdom. It grew out of older age-grade customs, which the Mthethwa chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a> began to use as military drafts and which Shaka reorganised radically. Young men were formed into regiments by age rather than clan, served the king until he allowed them to marry, and fought with the short stabbing spear and large shield in the buffalo horns formation. The impi defeated the British at Isandlwana in 1879.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/impi.jpg" alt="Impi" width="170" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arch. Webb · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zuluchargegutt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before Shaka, warfare among the peoples of KwaZulu Natal was frequent but of low intensity, fought over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, personal insults or grazing land. Many battles were arranged in advance, with women and children watching from a distance, and consisted of ritual taunts, single combats and tentative charges by men armed with several thin throwing spears, the assegai, and small cowhide shields. Casualties were usually light, and the defeated might pay in land or cattle. The quick raid was the most frequent action.</p><p>In the early 19th century rising populations, white settlement and slaving, and ambitious new men changed this pattern. According to historians such as Donald Morris, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a> of the Mthethwa built a relatively light hegemony by diplomacy and judicious force that reduced feuding among the clans. Shaka, serving in his iziCwe regiment, designed the short stabbing spear, the iklwa, with a broad blade 25 centimetres long, and a larger shield, the isihlangu, and discarded sandals so that his men could run faster.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>After their 20th birthdays young men were sorted into amabutho and built their ikhanda, a stockade of huts around a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> corral, where they gathered when summoned. Boys trained from as early as six and went on campaign as porters, carrying rations, cooking pots and sleeping mats. A regiment might number 400 or 4000 men under an inDuna, and regiments were grouped into corps named after their military kraals. Each ibutho had its own headdress and shield colouring: junior regiments carried largely dark shields, senior ones lighter, and Shaka's own Fasimba white.</p><p>The impi attacked in the buffalo horns formation. The two horns, usually younger troops, encircled and pinned the enemy; the chest, the prime fighters, delivered the decisive blow; the loins, often older veterans, waited in reserve, sometimes with their backs to the battle. Encirclement was not new in the region; what set the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> apart was the degree of organisation, the consistency with which they used these tactics and the speed of their execution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Shaka's brother and successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane kaSenzangakhona</a>, reintroduced greater use of the throwing spear, perhaps against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> firearms. At Ndondakusuka, two decades before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> deployed 30,000 men. After Isandlwana the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulus</a> captured many Martini–Henry rifles but lacked training and used them ineffectively. The attacks on Rorke's Drift and Kambula, both bloody defeats, seem to have been made against Cetshwayo's orders, and massed attacks were broken by rifles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gatling-gun">Gatling guns</a> and artillery, finally at Ulindi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Impi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
